  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCurrent low stratus and foggy conditions will continue through\nthe night, with these conditions clearing up around the late \nmorning hours. Persistent northerly flow will keep some \nmoisture around, and cloudy skies will likely linger well into \nthe afternoon before drier air moves in from the north. Without\nthe any rain expected today, and some breaks in the clouds\nthrough the morning, temperatures are expected to climb into \nthe 60s across the region. Clearing skies overnight will lead to\ngood radiational cooling and fog development. There is \npotential for fog to become widespread across the region. \n\nA ridge builds into the region tomorrow. Southwesterly flow \nwill advect in warmer air, helping push temperatures into the \n70s and even 80s through Thursday. Nighttime temperatures will \nbe more mild as well, only falling into the 50s and 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next chance for rain across the region comes with a frontal \nboundary late in the week. This system approaches the region \nThursday night and begins to slowly move through on Friday. The \nfront eventually departs the region on Saturday with high pressure \nand cooler air filling in behind. The high pressure quickly slides \noff to the east, allowing for a low pressure system to move up into \nthe Northeast on Sunday. Additional showers are expected with this \nsystem to round out the weekend. Unlike this week's Nor'easter, this \nlow pressure system will be quicker to move through with potential \nfor drier conditions to start next week.\n\nWith both of these systems, there are uncertainties. The arrival of \nthe frontal system still varies by 6 to 12 hours between guidance. \nModels have also trended slightly slower with this system. While \nguidance have come into better agreement with the timing of Sunday's \nsystem, there is still uncertainty on the track and how it interacts \nwith a low in Canada.",
